{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ad5c410ce0348e8b","type":"text","text":"# Obiettivo\nModellare l'interazione proteina ligando senza ignorare la flessibilità della tasca di legame\n\n# Il problema\nUn modello mentale piu accurato per descrivere la sfida è: 'progettare una chiave per un meccanismo che cambia forma'\n\nTrovare la chiave: data una proteina trovare il ligando che massimizza l'affinità di legame\n\nWorst offenders: GPCRs and kinases - pharmacologically critical, highly flexible\n\n# Solution\nToday's talk is about a new approach that lets the protein move throughout the entire design process.","x":120,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"7e38f0b17822747d","type":"text","text":"# Introduction\n\n## The method","x":620,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"b021dddb4c49ffe6","type":"text","text":"# Introduzione\n\nUnpack the title: De novo protein-ligand design including protein flexibility and conformational adaptation.\n\n","x":1160,"y":-1140,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"08ae0c373a14131d","type":"text","text":"# L'oracolo imperfetto\nPerché funzioni nel loop Monte Carlo, l'oracolo deve fare **tre cose**:\n\n1. Prendere in input sequenza proteica + SMILES del ligando\n2. Costruire da zero il complesso 3D (così la tasca si adatta al ligando — induced fit)\n3. Restituire un punteggio di confidenza che correli almeno _in media_ con l'affinità di legame\n\n[[Figure1.CorrelationChaiScoreVsExperimentalBindingAffinity.png]]\n\nThe Chai-1 pLDDT score is a *proxy* for affinity, not a rigorous free energy. Figure 1 shows the positive average trend is noisy — a single molecule with high pLDDT can have weak experimental affinity. Classical methods with careful calibration can be more precise on well-characterised targets; Chai-1 trades precision for speed and generality.","x":1160,"y":-820,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"4a2de7769061a400","type":"text","text":"# La logica argomentativa\n\nIl paper si regge su una catena logica precisa:\n\n1. **Premessa metodologica:** AI-MCLig usa Chai-1 come oracolo di scoring in un loop Monte Carlo — ad ogni step accetta o rifiuta una modifica del ligando in base al punteggio Chai-1.\n2. **Problema:** Chai-1 restituisce un \"confidence score\" (derivato da pLDDT), che misura quanto il modello è sicuro della struttura predetta. **Non è una misura diretta di affinità di legame.**\n3. **Domanda critica:** ha senso massimizzare la confidence di Chai-1 se vogliamo trovare ligandi che leghino _forte_?\n4. **Risposta (Figura 1):** sì, perché in media più alta è la confidence, più forte è l'affinità sperimentale. **Senza questa correlazione, l'intero metodo non avrebbe fondamento.**","x":1160,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"58c4a37f9df0f987","type":"text","text":"# Base","x":-360,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":300},
		{"id":"c5eed595d005b672","type":"text","text":"# Introduction\n\n## The problem\n\n*Designing drugs means finding small molecules that bind to a specific protein — but proteins are not rigid locks.*\n\n- Molti farmaci sono piccole molecole organiche che legano una proteina target, alterandone l'effetto.\n- Trovare molecole che legano la tasca di legame in uno spazio di possibilità chimiche virtualmente infinito\n- Traditional methods assume a fixed binding pocket ignoring induced fit, a critical limitation\n  %% Worst offenders: GPCRs and Kinases not only are highly flexible but are especially relevant in pharmacology %%\n\n## The opportunity\n\n*AI structure predictors make protein flexibility available at every evaluation step — this is the paper's core insight.*\n\n- AF successors can predict protein-ligand complex from seq+SMILE in seconds\n- To explore this vast space of possibilities with a stochastic search guided by an oracle","x":160,"y":880,"width":640,"height":460},
		{"id":"1b189544a422f5a0","type":"text","text":"# Base","x":-520,"y":880,"width":640,"height":460},
		{"id":"0ad14b92b38ce5e0","type":"text","text":"# MC Simulation Approach\n*AI-MCLig grows a ligand from benzene by applying random chemical operations, accepting or rejecting each change based on how much it improves the Chai-1 score.*\n\n- Start from benzene (the simplest aromatic scaffold) as the initial [[SMILES]]\n- At each step: apply one chemical operation from a **moveset**\n- Rebuild the full protein–ligand complex with [[Chai-1]] from scratch → get new [[pLDDT]] score\n- Accept/reject using the [[Metropolis acceptance criterion]]: $P(\\Delta s) = \\exp(-\\beta \\cdot \\Delta s)$","x":820,"y":880,"width":720,"height":460},
		{"id":"087397d4e0c9cdb8","type":"text","text":"# Ask us something\n[[Why Chai-1 as oracle rather than formula-based physical eval of energy state]]\n[[Why those probabilities for each move]]\n[[Metropolis acceptance criterion |What is beta? ]]\n[[Ligand vs binder |Difference between ligand and binder]]\n[[Why start with benzene?]]\n[[Is high affinity always the functional requirement?]]\n[[It looks like once plateau is reached (250+ moves) the MC engine is inefficient]]\n\tHow to fix it? Set higher inverse temperature, Create fine-works moveset\n","x":-520,"y":400,"width":640,"height":440},
		{"id":"e7142248ae370ba1","type":"text","text":"# Movesets\n\n## 9 Atomistic moves $\\beta$ = 50 (less exploratory)\n*Nine chemical operations cover the full range of atom-level edits; their probabilities were empirically tuned on three protein targets.*\n\n//Add image\n\n## BRICS based moves $\\beta$ = 5 (more exploratory)\n*Instead of atom-level edits, the fragment-based variant recombines whole molecular building blocks — larger jumps through chemical space.*","x":1560,"y":880,"width":720,"height":460},
		{"id":"882b82fe4db6f19d","type":"text","text":"# Scoring of compounds dunring MC Simulation\n*The score is not just Chai-1 — three drug-property biases steer the search toward synthesisable, soluble, drug-like molecules.*\n\n$$\\text{score} = 0.8 \\cdot \\text{Chai-1} - 0.1 \\cdot \\text{SA} + 0.05 \\cdot \\text{ESOL} + 0.05 \\cdot \\text{QED}$$\n\n- **Chai-1** (weight 0.8): [[pLDDT]] confidence of the predicted complex — proxy for binding affinity\n- **SA** (weight −0.1): [[Synthetic accessibility score]] — penalises hard-to-synthesise molecules; capped at a maximum threshold (a ligand must be complex enough to fill the pocket)\n- **ESOL** (weight +0.05): estimated aqueous solubility (Delaney 2004) — positive contribution favours water-soluble compounds\n- **QED** (weight +0.05): quantitative estimate of drug-likeness (Bickerton et al. 2012, without MW factor) — favours Lipinski-compliant molecules\n\nNote: Lower coeff for chai-1 will lead to failed attempts of score maximization (across all 4 targets)\n\n//Reason why it's usefull to incorporate the drug-likeness at this stage rather then filter later\n//A. To avoid wasted compute","x":2320,"y":880,"width":720,"height":460},
		{"id":"f1cd15f81d25270a","type":"text","text":"# Molecular Dynamics simulations and MMGBSA Calculations\n- _\"Per validare i composti generati, gli autori usano MMGBSA, un metodo fisico-chimico classico che calcola l'energia di legame da una simulazione di dinamica molecolare di 20 nanosecondi.\"_\n- _\"Il punto chiave è l'indipendenza concettuale: Chai-1 ha guidato il design, ma MMGBSA è basato su fisica diversa — quindi una concordanza tra i due è un segnale forte.\"_\n- _\"Il risultato: i composti generati de novo hanno energie di legame nello stesso range dei leganti sperimentalmente noti. Questa è la prova fisica che AI-MCLig non sta generando rumore.\"_\n\n## What\nStima l'energia libera di legame\nmetodo basato su simulazione MD + solvatazione implicita\nPiù negativo = legame più forte. \nRange tipico per farmaci: **−15 a −50 kcal/mol**.\n\n## Why\nChai-1 è un'AI che dà un punteggio di confidenza strutturale. MMGBSA è fisica classica (campo di forze + termodinamica)\nDue metodi con assunzioni diverse che concordano makes for stronger evidence of correctness\n\n# The result\nI composti generati de novo mostrano valori MMGBSA tra −19 e −57 kcal/mol — nello stesso range dei leganti sperimentalmente noti (Tabella 3).","x":3080,"y":880,"width":780,"height":460},
		{"id":"9b3d1d816b4c4ca0","x":3880,"y":880,"width":800,"height":460,"type":"text","text":"Results and discussion\n# MC-based recovery of target ligands from simple starting compounds\n*Does the chemichal space engine work?: replacing Chai-1 with dice similarity to a known ligand, the MC search rediscovers the target compound from benzene in most cases.*\n\nValidation strategy: swap the oracle scores for [[Dice Similarity vs Chai-1 Scoring |dice-similarity]] to a known target compound — if the search works, it should recover the target.\n\n[[section-results-recovery#^072456|Can MC find treasure if given perfect hints? Yes 8/11]]\nExplain table 1\n[[section-results-recovery#^baf092 |Explain local minimum issue]]\n[[section-results-recovery#^dd01c8|Why not just raise the temperature then?]]"},
		{"id":"dec3f1b412357a5c","x":3880,"y":1380,"width":800,"height":400,"type":"text","text":"# Abount these validations\nE' importante notare che queste due validazioni non sono ridondanti ma complementari.\n\n|Validazione|Cosa testa|Quando avviene|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Recovery test (Tabella 1)**|\"Funziona il **motore di ricerca**?\"|Prima del design vero|\n|**MMGBSA + Boltz-2 (Tabella 2)**|\"I composti generati **sono leganti plausibili**?\"|Dopo il design vero|"},
		{"id":"1bb9628e89581c25","x":4700,"y":880,"width":740,"height":460,"type":"text","text":"Results and discussion\n# Correlation of Chai-1 confidence score and ligand binding affinity\n\n# Chai-1 the imperfect oracle\n*Chai-1 pLDDT is not a direct affinity measurement — but its average trend is positive, and that is enough to guide a Monte Carlo search uphill.*\n\n- Three protein targets evaluated: bromodomain, [[Pim-1 kinase]], [[p38 kinase]]\n- Experimental binding affinities (kcal/mol) from PDBbind (Liu et al. 2017) compared to Chai-1 pLDDT scores\n- **Key result**: no direct point-to-point correlation; but the **average trend** across binned Chai-1 scores is consistently positive for all three targets\n\n//Figure1\n[[section-results-chai1-affinity#^65a0bf|*The oracle is imperfect but good enough to guide the search*]]\n"},
		{"id":"ea01f35c7193047b","x":5460,"y":880,"width":700,"height":460,"type":"text","text":"Results and discussion\n# De novo generation of bound ligands using atomistic-step MC simulation\n\n*From benzene to a drug-like compound in 2 000 steps: AI-MCLig generates novel ligands whose MMGBSA and Boltz-2 scores match experimentally known binders.*\n\n- Applied to four targets: bromodomain, [[p38 kinase]], [[Pim-1 kinase]], β-1 adrenergic receptor (GPCR)\n- 2 000 MC steps, $\\beta = 50$, starting from benzene; **3 independent runs per target** (~20 h each on NVIDIA RTX4090)\n- Final Chai-1 scores: **0.85–0.92** across all 12 compounds (Table 2)\n- MMGBSA scores: **−19 to −57 kcal/mol** — in the same range as experimentally validated high-affinity binders (Table 3)\n\n//Show figure 2"},
		{"id":"7f8cb1a3558b3afb","x":5460,"y":1360,"width":700,"height":420,"type":"text","text":"Results and discussion\n# De novo generation of bound ligands using atomistic-step MC simulation\n\n- Score improves rapidly in the **first 250 steps**, then more gradually (Figure 3A)\n- Pocket RMSD (Figure 3C): **bromodomain** stays low (~0.5 Å) — rigid pocket; **p38 kinase** and **Pim-1 kinase** show larger fluctuations (0.5–2.0 Å) — flexible pockets adapting to each candidate ligand\n- Cleanup phase: 100 additional steps optimising SA, ESOL, QED with Chai-1 threshold constraint — improves drug-likeness without losing binding score\n- UMAP (supplementary Fig. 14): 3 [[UMAP (uniform manifold approximation and projection)#^e19b01|structurally diverse outputs per target all land near known-binder clusters in chemical space]]"},
		{"id":"dc69695c0416cda9","x":6200,"y":880,"width":760,"height":460,"type":"text","text":"# Conclusions\n*AI-MCLig demonstrates that a simple Monte Carlo search scored by an AI structure predictor is sufficient to generate novel drug-like ligands with predicted binding energies comparable to known experimental binders.*\n\n- **Central claim**: [[Chai-1]] used as a scoring oracle inside a [[Monte Carlo simulation]] loop enables *de novo* ligand generation with **full protein flexibility** — no rigid pocket assumption\n- **Validated on 4 targets**: bromodomain, [[p38 kinase]], [[Pim-1 kinase]], β-1 adrenergic receptor (GPCR) — diverse target classes\n- **MMGBSA and Boltz-2 scores** of generated compounds overlap with experimentally known high-affinity binders (Tables 2 & 3)\n- **Complementary, not competing**: the approach supplements traditional docking and deep generative models; can be adapted to optimise side chains only, fix a scaffold, or use fragment recombination"}
	],
	"edges":[]
}